 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c \\\\ d\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V=\\text{Col}(A)$ spanned by the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ of the matrix $A$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ shown above. Given that ${a}_{1}⊥{a}_{2},$ and that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the first two vectors are already orthogonal, we proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: We set ${v}_{2}={a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3**. Next, we find ${v}_{3}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{3}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{4}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{6}{10}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{3}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(5[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{(-\\frac{2}{5})}{(-\\frac{1}{5})}=2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-41626/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "866ab12b-7f51-55f8-9e7b-e39f9c3b4b44"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-41626"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-40772"] :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c \\\\ d\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V=\\text{Col}(A)$ spanned by the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ of the matrix $A$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ shown above. Given that ${a}_{1}⊥{a}_{2},$ and that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{d}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the first two vectors are already orthogonal, we proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: We set ${v}_{2}={a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3**. Next, we find ${v}_{3}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{3}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-(-\\frac{6}{4})[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{2}{12}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(6[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+9[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{d}=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{3})}{(-\\frac{2}{3})}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-140895/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "0ad6fd62-c8ba-58ae-a59b-712470bd9ec6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140895/selection/25666b67a56594dd5245ec05e89d883db07d04362ab51dbc90c39ad20f7f9142" :answer/id #uuid "f1a116f6-04cd-5c7c-ac30-9edba525cf14" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140895/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "bb8a46f7-2844-5369-86b3-4cd4038e8124" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140895/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "3bde79a3-3c0f-5424-9fd2-6c1820e74303" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140895/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "4147b0e3-fe3e-5627-8797-2af0063f66e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140895/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "721a0f58-f640-53cc-b917-421538655e83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-140895/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb8