-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2.** We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{6}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We find the vector ${v}_{3}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{6}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{12}{12}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{(-1)}{(-2)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-95862/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9870ad37-3e54-5403-936c-09a321d4a0cf"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-95862"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-40818"] :question/problem "$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}],\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {v}_{1} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]\\,,[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]\\,,[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nSuppose that $V=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$ Given that $B$ is an orthogonal basis for $V,$ and that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "We find an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows:\n\n**Step 1.** We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2.** We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{10}{5}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We find the vector ${v}_{3}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{15}{5}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{46}{46}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -15\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=1,$ $b=-2,$ and $\\frac{a}{b}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-41631"] :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {v}_{1} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]\\,,[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]\\,,[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c \\\\ d\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V=\\text{Col}(A)$ spanned by the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ of the matrix $A,$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ shown above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{d}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed to find an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows:\n\n**Step 1.** We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2.** We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{2}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We find the vector ${v}_{3}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{2}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{10}{20}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\frac{d}{a}=\\frac{(-\\frac{3}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=-3.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-140826/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "438f7b75-9775-5ff3-9f2e-34cda4df7fb5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140826/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "baa2f2fd-7713-5344-8340-b4107bf8ce2e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140826/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "b7835210-dedc-53b6-8905-8855476bf7a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140826/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "1263b21f-7cdd-5374-bf3e-b76171e24eb7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140826/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "f6de57e2-6353-5420-9a8f-82c3e953fcf7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140826/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "fdc7cb29-d648-58d0-bcd2-d40642f83dce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-140826/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-140826/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-140826/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-140826/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-140826/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-140826/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-140826" :question/id #uuid "937e65ec-4f62-5243-829a-bd84cd77153c" :question/math-academy-id "q-140826" :question/problem "$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {v}_{1} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]\\,,[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]\\,,[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nSuppose that $V=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$ Given that $B$ is an orthogonal basis for $V,$ and that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "We find an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows:\n\n**Step 1.** We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2.** We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We find the vector ${v}_{3}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=-4,$ $b=-2,$ and $\\frac{a}{b}=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-140826/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9870ad37-3e54-5403-936c-09a321d4a0cf"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140826"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5584"] :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072057/assets/26a4907b9698a976361fbf6396c09a56edc417f177e512a82f6fb1495624f98b.png)\n\nSuppose that $V = \\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf a_1, \\mathbf a_2, \\mathbf a_3\\}.$ Given that $\\mathcal B = \\{\\mathbf a_1, \\mathbf a_2, \\mathbf b\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V, \\mathbf{a}_1 \\perp \\mathbf{a}_2,$ and that $\\mathcal B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\dfrac{c}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the first two vectors are already orthogonal, we proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: We set ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072057/assets/ea56a2ffc9a7f267e5de0cace94d505050e7df7ade72986b0b8e5ae6c91e7385.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We set ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072057/assets/a87589c86b41d3fd44e6346b300a103c16301bb6cde410084a794b5430b3599a.png)\n\n**Step 3**. Next, we find $\\mathbf{v}_3$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf{v}_3$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-(-\\frac{2}{4})[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{0}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=-\\dfrac 1 2,$ $c=-\\dfrac 3 2,$ and $\\dfrac{c}{a} = \\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}{\\left(-\\dfrac 1 2\\right)} = 3.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8321"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072057/assets/a0a83c2b8fe6a0ccf1e35f49f239e51c256ce3d19ddfe86ba9b215ddd1d3579b.png)\n\nConsider the subspace $V=\\textrm{Col}(A)$ spanned by the columns $\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3$ of the matrix $A,$ and an **orthonormal** basis $\\mathcal{B}$ of $\\textrm{Col}(A),$ shown above. Given that $\\mathbf{a}_1 \\perp \\mathbf{a}_2,$ and that $\\mathcal B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|\\,d\\,|?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the first two vectors are already orthogonal, we proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072057/assets/fac944c1f224d01dc33db95a5971ff47483e04ecf06492d8cfecb44d05de225c.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We set ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072057/assets/ce3195325db5c00042ec58cb51b8e17c77df742112ea7ad6481920647a6c77bb.png)\n\n**Step 3**. Next, we find $\\mathbf{v}_3$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf{v}_3$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{10}{10}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{20}{20}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize $\\mathbf{v}_3$ dividing it by $\\|\\mathbf{v}_3\\| = \\sqrt{10}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{3} & =\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $|\\,d\\,| = \\left|-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\right|=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5585"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072057/assets/1e6a41f2d6f834b7f9b8c937d19a4f47d45ee6bf2705d783e9a254e8478df15d.png)\n\nSuppose that $V = \\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf a_1, \\mathbf a_2, \\mathbf a_3\\}.$ Given that $\\mathcal B$ is an orthogonal basis for $V,$ and that $\\mathcal B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\dfrac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "We find an orthogonal basis $\\mathcal B$ of $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows:\n\n**Step 1.** We set ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072057/assets/3f7fd351c90e195ff46d0fdec20b2bda8a5a784c17a97700238fab0b19ebe836.png)\n\n**Step 2.** We find the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1\\}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{3}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We find the vector $\\mathbf{v}_3$ orthogonal to $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-(-\\frac{3}{3})[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{3}{6}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $b=\\dfrac 3 2,$ $c=-\\dfrac 3 2,$ and $\\dfrac{b}{c} = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}{\\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}=-1.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
